      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm, which involves repeated division and taking remainders. Let's go through the steps:\n\n1. **Divide 1407 by 903 and find the remainder:**\n   \\[\n   1407 \\div 903 = 1 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1407 - 903 \\times 1 = 504 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n   So, \\(1407 = 903 \\times 1 + 504\\).\n\n2. **Now, divide 903 by 504 and find the remainder:**\n   \\[\n   903 \\div 504 = 1 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   903 - 504 \\times 1 = 399 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n   So, \\(903 = 504 \\times 1 + 399\\).\n\n3. **Next, divide 504 by 399 and find the remainder:**\n   \\[\n   504 \\div 399 = 1 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   504 - 399 \\times 1 = 105 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n   So, \\(504 = 399 \\times 1 + 105\\).\n\n4. **Then, divide 399 by 105 and find the remainder:**\n   \\[\n   399 \\div 105 = 3 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   399 - 105 \\times 3 = 48 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n   So, \\(399 = 105 \\times 3 + 48\\).\n\n5. **Next, divide 105 by 48 and find the remainder:**\n   \\[\n   105 \\div 48 = 2 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   105 - 48 \\times 2 = 6 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n   So, \\(105 = 48 \\times 2 + 6\\).\n\n6. **Then, divide 48 by 6 and find the remainder:**\n   \\[\n   48 \\div 6 = 8 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   48 - 6 \\times 8 = 0 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n   So, \\(48 = 6 \\times 8 + 0\\).\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the divisor at this step, which is 6, is the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903.\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
